WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:07.490
All of these pictures: the sun-dappled landscapes
and surreal undersea worlds, the abstract

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:12.680
sculptures and slightly-hazy futures were
all made...here.

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:15.610
In the video game called Minecraft.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:21.190
It’s the type of art that gets collected
in coffee table books

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:24.060
and gets made by design collectives.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:29.949
With more than 100 million copies sold, Minecraft
has become more than a game.

00:00:29.949 --> 00:00:33.320
This game has become an artistic medium.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:35.760
And it is also a business.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.890
But even though Minecraft is a liberating
game,

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:44.750
building a business in a virtual world
can be just as tricky as building it in the

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:46.000
real one.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:56.410
“At first I thought, OK, you know, the graphics
aren’t great, but I had a sort of childhood

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:59.480
fascination with Lego.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:05.600
About two weeks I got bored with the whole
survival aspect and then started looking at

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.200
this thing called creative mode.”

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.360
James Delaney is from London, and he’s one
of the people who runs Blockworks, an artistic

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.100
collective and company.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.170
He’s talking about how Minecraft works.

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:21.960
Players choose between two different modes:
one is survival mode, which is a little like

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:27.909
a traditional video game, since scary spiders
and other creatures come out at night.

00:01:27.909 --> 00:01:29.869
There’s also creative mode.

00:01:29.869 --> 00:01:35.549
It is a blank canvas, in which players can
design unique worlds.

00:01:35.549 --> 00:01:41.509
It really is like making something out of
Legos - you arrange a variety of digital blocks

00:01:41.509 --> 00:01:44.950
with tons of options to increase the complexity.

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:48.479
As Minecraft grew, a new industry grew with
it.

00:01:48.479 --> 00:01:53.229
To play Minecraft online, players choose from
a bunch of different servers that can host

00:01:53.229 --> 00:01:54.229
the game.

00:01:54.229 --> 00:01:58.689
In the beginning, these servers weren’t
owned by Mojang, the company that created

00:01:58.689 --> 00:01:59.689
Minecraft.

00:01:59.689 --> 00:02:05.540
So as different servers tried to attract players,
they added lots of bells and whistles, like

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:10.270
intriguing worlds, or maps, and even their
own mods of the game.

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:12.370
An industry was born.

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:17.400
All sorts of companies and guilds sprung up
to make Minecraft maps.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.440
Just like players, they were located all around
the world.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:25.030
“We have another director, Sean Davidson,
who’s over in Canada.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:28.690
We have 62 members from over 20 different
countries.

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:32.870
But everything’s done remotely, so there’s
no office with more than one person in it.”

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:37.980
Their company, Blockworks, started with maps
for servers, but the artistic potential kept

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:38.980
them going.

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:44.840
Though they might beautify their work using
plugins and textures, all of it is made in

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:50.800
game, with obsessive detail that fans can
download and appreciate for themselves.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:56.550
They create beautiful surreal worlds, like
a map about...Minecraft, the game, or a beautiful

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:58.170
dreamscape of clouds.

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:04.709
“I started thinking about this city which
would be constructed from giant musical instruments

00:03:04.709 --> 00:03:08.239
with a very sort of mechanical steampunk theme.”

00:03:08.239 --> 00:03:14.190
As Minecraft grew, big companies saw potential
to promote their stuff through Minecraft maps.

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:19.560
Blockworks scored tie-ins for movies like
Tomorrowland and Batman v. Superman.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.830
They even made a Batmobile.

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:30.740
But as unusual as it sounds, building ads
inside virtual worlds is not a new idea.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:36.340
This is Chex Quest, a 1996 game built on top
of Doom’s engine.

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:39.620
People got it from a CD-Rom inside Chex boxes.

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:44.080
At the time, Doom was considered an ultraviolent
video game.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:46.260
So they remade it with…

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:47.260
Chex.

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:52.549
For a small fee, Chex licensed the software
and built a world inside a world.

00:03:52.549 --> 00:03:57.400
That trend of virtual world ads continued
in Second Life, a platform launched in the

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:00.379
2000s that’s still going today.

00:04:00.379 --> 00:04:05.000
Companies from Reebok to American Apparel
built virtual stores there, earning a lot

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:06.049
of press.

00:04:06.049 --> 00:04:10.939
But as the platform started to decline in
popularity, the headlines disappeared.

00:04:10.939 --> 00:04:16.180
Minecraft, however, might have the biggest
opportunities and risks.

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:23.150
In May 2016, Minecraft and Mojang’s current
owner, Microsoft, declared that branded partnerships

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:27.210
- like the glorious Batmobile - were no longer
allowed.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:30.680
Microsoft said it “didn’t feel right,
or fun.”

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:38.840
It also meant they wanted to retain control
of how money was made off Minecraft maps.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:43.910
Blockworks made out OK — Microsoft hires
them to make gorgeous maps like the seven

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:45.639
wonders of the ancient world.

00:04:45.639 --> 00:04:51.000
They stayed in the club when other mapmakers
got kicked out of the branded map game.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:54.410
“We’re lucky enough to work with Microsoft,
and obviously they’re exempt from their

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:55.410
own guidelines.

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:59.030
I think a lot of other teams did struggle
as a result of that.

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:00.669
And it closed a lot of doors.

00:05:00.669 --> 00:05:06.430
At the same time, it’s quite understandable
that Microsoft want to keep control over how

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:08.389
their platform is used.”

00:05:08.389 --> 00:05:11.250
Collectives like James’s might not mind
that much.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:16.590
In addition to Microsoft gigs, they have artistic
projects and historical collaborations.

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:20.810
“So this was commissioned by the Museum
of London, it ties in with and exhibition

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:23.740
they’ve got commemorating the Great Fire
of London in 1666.”

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:30.139
But the change in rules shows the risks of
building your business in another person’s

00:05:30.139 --> 00:05:31.139
world.

00:05:31.139 --> 00:05:36.850
Promotional map crackdowns, and even limits
on private servers, have kept the Minecraft

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:39.050
economy kind of uncertain.

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:44.539
When you’re playing another person’s game,
night could come at any time.

00:05:44.539 --> 00:05:51.509
And then, it’s always survival mode.

00:05:51.509 --> 00:05:56.120
We put links to all the maps we used in this
video in the description, so there’s a lot

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:57.960
to explore and have fun with.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:01.070
Now I’m gonna put this gold block on the
ground.

00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:01.330
OK.

